    "id": "medqbank-pathology-0020",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 20,
    "stem": "Which of the following best describes\nthe pathophysiology of Meckel\ndiverticulum?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Congenital failure of duodenum\nto canalize"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Failure of vitelline duct to\ninvolute"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Damaged ganglion cells in the\nmyenteric plexus"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Congenital hypertrophy of\npyloric smooth muscle"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum (involving all bowel\nwall layers) caused by failure of the vitelline duct to involute.\nFollows the Rule of 2s:\n• Occurs in 2% of the population\n• 2 inches long\n• Located within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve\n• May present by age 2\nOther choices:\n(Choice A) Congenital failure of duodenum to canalize results\nin duodenal atresia\n(Choice C) Damaged ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus is\ncharacteristic of achalasia\n(Choice D) Congenital hypertrophy of pyloric smooth\nmuscle results in pyloric stenosis",
