    "id": "medqbank-pathology-0079",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 79,
    "stem": "Which of the following is the key\nmediator in activating macrophages to\ncontain M. tuberculosis infection?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "TNF-α"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "IL-12"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "IFN-γ"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "IL-1"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "IFN-γ serves as the critical mediator in activating\nmacrophages for containing M. tuberculosis infection, it does\nso through the following mechanisms:\n• Phagolysosome maturation: IFN-γ activates\nphagolysosome maturation, exposing bacteria to an\nacidic, oxidizing environment.\n• Nitric oxide production: Stimulates inducible nitric\noxide synthase (iNOS) to produce NO, which forms\nreactive nitrogen intermediates for mycobacterial\nkilling.\n• Antimicrobial peptides: Mobilizes defensins to\ncombat bacteria.\n• Autophagy: Triggers autophagy to destroy damaged\norganelles and intracellular bacteria like M.\ntuberculosis.",
