    "explanation": "Vaginal adenosis occurs when columnar epithelium in the\nupper vagina persists instead of being replaced by squamous\nepithelium during development.\nIt is more common in females exposed to diethylstilbestrol\n(DES) in utero.\nVaginal adenosis can give rise to clear cell\nadenocarcinoma, which is the persistence of glandular\ncolumnar epithelium in the proximal vagina.\n(Choice A) Arsenic is a carcinogen that is associated with the\nfollowing malignancies:\n• Hepatic angiosarcoma\n• Lung cancer\n• Squamous cell carcinoma of the skin\n(Choice B) Vinyl chloride is a carcinogen that is associated\nwith hepatic angiosarcoma.\n(Choice D) Benzene is a substance that is associated\nwith aplastic anemia. Other drugs associated with aplastic\nanemia are:\n• Carbamazepine\n• Methimazole\n• NSAIDs\n• Benzene\n• Chloramphenicol\n• Propylthiouracil\n✎ High Yield Links:\nDiethylstilbestrol (DES) is a teratogen that may cause\nvaginal adenosis, vaginal clear cell adenocarcinoma, and\ncongenital Müllerian anomalies in patients who had in-utero\nexposure.\nThe table below summarizes the teratogenic medications and\ntheir effects on the fetus:",
