    "id": "notion-pathology-0004",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 4,
    "stem": "A two-year old female presented with a defect in her lower anterior abdomen wall since birth. On physical examination, you noted a globular soft tissue protrusion from the defect. In the inferior aspect of which were two openings where urine dribbled continuously. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Exstrophy"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Congenital diverticulum"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Congenital diaphragmatic hernia"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Persistent urachus"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "In classic bladder exstrophy, the bladder protrudes from the abdominal wall and its mucosa is exposed. The umbilicus is displaced downward, the pubic rami are widely separated in the midline, and the rectus muscles are separated. The consequences of untreated bladder exstrophy are total urinary incontinence and an increased incidence of bladder cancer, usually adenocarcinoma.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "In classic bladder exstrophy, the bladder protrudes from the abdominal wall and its mucosa is exposed. The umbilicus is displaced downward, the pubic rami are widely separated in the midline, and the rectus muscles are separated. The consequences of untreated bladder exstrophy are total urinary incontinence and an increased incidence of bladder cancer, usually adenocarcinoma.",
      "B": "Bladder diverticula develop as herniations of bladder mucosa between defects of bladder smooth muscle fibers. Primary bladder diverticula usually develop at the ureterovesical junction and may be associated with vesicoureteral reflux.",
      "C": "Congenital diaphragmatic hernia is a communication between the abdominal and thoracic cavities with or without abdominal contents in the thorax. Respiratory distress is a cardinal sign. They may also have a scaphoid abdomen and increased chest wall diameter.",
      "D": "Urachal abnormalities are more common in boys than in girls. A patent urachus can occur as an isolated anomaly or associated with prune-belly syndrome or posterior urethral valves. In this condition, there is continuous urinary drainage from the umbilicus."
    },
