    "id": "notion-pathology-0018",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 18,
    "stem": "Which of the following lung pathology can give rise to pulmonary embolism?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Abnormalities in chest walls"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Defect in pulmonary airways"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Defects in lung parenchyma"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Defect in pulmonary vasculature"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Pulmonary embolism usually occurs in patients with a predisposing condition that produces an increased tendency to clot (thrombophilia). Patients often have cardiac disease or cancer, or have been immobilized for several days or weeks prior to the appearance of asymptomatic embolism. Those with hip fractures are at particularly high risk. Hypercoagulable states, either primary (e.g., factor V Leiden, prothrombin mutations, and antiphospholipid syndrome) or secondary (e.g., obesity, recent surgery, cancer, oral contraceptive use, pregnancy), are important risk factors. Indwelling central venous lines can be a nidus for formation of right atrial thrombi, which can embolize to the lungs. Rarely, pulmonary embolism may consist of fat, air, or tumor. Small bone marrow emboli are often seen in patients who die after chest compressions performed during resuscitative efforts. Large-vessel pulmonary thromboses are rare and develop only in the presence of pulmonary hypertension, pulmonary atherosclerosis, and heart failure.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Abnormalities in the chest walls, such as structural deformities or trauma, are not direct causes of pulmonary embolism. Pulmonary embolism is typically associated with the obstruction of pulmonary blood vessels by blood clots or other materials.",
      "B": "Defects in pulmonary airways, such as those seen in conditions like chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), do not directly lead to pulmonary embolism. Pulmonary embolism is more closely related to blood clot formation and embolization within the pulmonary vasculature.",
      "C": "Defects in lung parenchyma, such as those seen in conditions like pulmonary fibrosis, are not direct causes of pulmonary embolism. Pulmonary embolism involves the obstruction of pulmonary arteries by emboli, usually blood clots or other substances.",
      "D": "Pulmonary embolism usually occurs in patients with a predisposing condition that produces an increased tendency to clot (thrombophilia). Patients often have cardiac disease or cancer, or have been immobilized for several days or weeks prior to the appearance of asymptomatic embolism. Those with hip fractures are at particularly high risk. Hypercoagulable states, either primary (e.g., factor V Leiden, prothrombin mutations, and antiphospholipid syndrome) or secondary (e.g., obesity, recent surgery, cancer, oral contraceptive use, pregnancy), are important risk factors. Indwelling central venous lines can be a nidus for formation of right atrial thrombi, which can embolize to the lungs. Rarely, pulmonary embolism may consist of fat, air, or tumor. Small bone marrow emboli are often seen in patients who die after chest compressions performed during resuscitative efforts. Large-vessel pulmonary thromboses are rare and develop only in the presence of pulmonary hypertension, pulmonary atherosclerosis, and heart failure."
    },
