    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Presence of circulating anti-glomerular basement membrane antibodies"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Pathology is currently unknown or is idiopathic"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Renal pathology secondary to adaptive changes from systemic disease"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Diffuse inflammatory response after a severe or systemic infection"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN) is a syndrome of progressive loss of renal function, characterized by nephritic syndrome often with severe oliguria. Its histologic hallmark is the presence of crescents, thus crescentic GN. There are three types according to underlying immune mechanism:\nType I - Presence of Antibodies against glomerular BM with common diseases associated such as anti GBM disease and Goodpasture syndrome\nType II - Sequelae of immune-complex mediated glomerular injury, including diseases: PSAGN, Lupus Nephritis, HSP, Buerger disease\nType III - Pauci-immune (presence of ANCA) as seen in ANCA Vasculitides\nIn this problem, there is a concomitant diffuse pulmonary hemorrhage as seen in Goodpasture syndrome. Usual clinical picture is a male in his 20s who is an active smoker and presents with hemoptysis and renal failure. The pathology behind this - antibodies against the noncollagenous domain of alpha-3 chain of collagen type IV leading to necrotizing hemorrhagic interstitial pneumonitis and RPGN.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN) is a syndrome of progressive loss of renal function, characterized by nephritic syndrome often with severe oliguria. Its histologic hallmark is the presence of crescents, thus crescentic GN. There are three types according to underlying immune mechanism:\nType I - Presence of Antibodies against glomerular BM with common diseases associated such as anti GBM disease and Goodpasture syndrome\nType II - Sequelae of immune-complex mediated glomerular injury, including diseases: PSAGN, Lupus Nephritis, HSP, Buerger disease\nType III - Pauci-immune (presence of ANCA) as seen in ANCA Vasculitides\nIn this problem, there is a concomitant diffuse pulmonary hemorrhage as seen in Goodpasture syndrome. Usual clinical picture is a male in his 20s who is an active smoker and presents with hemoptysis and renal failure. The pathology behind this - antibodies against the noncollagenous domain of alpha-3 chain of collagen type IV leading to necrotizing hemorrhagic interstitial pneumonitis and RPGN.",
      "B": "While some cases of glomerulonephritis may have an unknown or idiopathic etiology, in the context of rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with diffuse pulmonary hemorrhage, an identifiable cause is often present. Goodpasture syndrome, as mentioned in option a, is one of the known causes.",
      "C": "Rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis is typically associated with immune complex-mediated or anti-GBM antibody-mediated glomerular injury. It is not commonly a result of adaptive changes from systemic diseases, and the presence of pulmonary hemorrhage suggests a more specific and aggressive process.",
      "D": "While severe or systemic infections can lead to glomerulonephritis, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis with concomitant diffuse pulmonary hemorrhage is not typically a direct consequence of a diffuse inflammatory response to infection. Goodpasture syndrome, with the presence of anti-GBM antibodies, is a more specific association."
    },
