    "id": "notion-pathology-0054",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 54,
    "stem": "What is the MAIN pathology involved in Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS)?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Preferential damage to myelin with relative preservation of axons, leading to poor transmission of electrical impulses"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Progressive loss of upper motor neurons in the cerebral cortex and lower motor neurons in the spinal cord and brainstem"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Protein accumulation and aggregation, mitochondrial abnormalities, and neuronal loss in the substantia nigra"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Characterized by cytoplasmic inclusions of alpha-synuclein in oligodendrocytes and degeneration of white matter"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "True. The hallmark pathology of ALS is the progressive degeneration of both upper motor neurons in the cerebral cortex and lower motor neurons in the spinal cord and brainstem. This results in muscle weakness and atrophy.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "False. ALS primarily involves the degeneration of motor neurons, not myelin. Myelin damage is more characteristic of demyelinating disorders like multiple sclerosis.",
      "B": "True. The hallmark pathology of ALS is the progressive degeneration of both upper motor neurons in the cerebral cortex and lower motor neurons in the spinal cord and brainstem. This results in muscle weakness and atrophy.",
      "C": "False. This description is more characteristic of Parkinson's disease, not ALS.",
      "D": "False. This description corresponds to disorders like multiple system atrophy, which involves the accumulation of alpha-synuclein, but not ALS."
    },
