    "id": "notion-pathology-0056",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 56,
    "stem": "Situation 1 - A 10-year old boy staying in the garbage mountain of Tondo, developed diarrhea for more than a week of about 5-6 times/ day. Stool was blood streaked and associated with mild abdominal tenderness. Patient also has intestinal tuberculosis. What organism is involved in this case?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Giardia lamblia"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Entamoeba histolytica"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Salmonella typhi"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Shigella sonnei"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Invasive (bloody) diarrhea — Shigellosis is the most common cause of invasive, or bloody, diarrhea among children in resource-limited countries. It is a significant contributor to mortality and is linked to a high incidence of bacteremia, seizures, and other life-threatening complications. Shigella dysenteriae, Shigella flexneri, Shigella boydii, and Shigella sonnei are the four species associated with this condition.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Unlikely. Giardia lamblia is a protozoan parasite that causes giardiasis. It typically presents with foul-smelling, greasy diarrhea, but blood streaking and abdominal tenderness are not characteristic features.",
      "B": "Possible. Entamoeba histolytica is a protozoan parasite that can cause amoebic dysentery with blood-streaked diarrhea and mild abdominal tenderness. Given the patient's living conditions, this organism is a consideration.",
      "C": "Unlikely. Salmonella typhi is associated with typhoid fever, which generally presents with sustained fever, abdominal pain, and constipation. Blood-streaked diarrhea is not a typical feature.",
      "D": "Invasive (bloody) diarrhea — Shigellosis is the most common cause of invasive, or bloody, diarrhea among children in resource-limited countries. It is a significant contributor to mortality and is linked to a high incidence of bacteremia, seizures, and other life-threatening complications. Shigella dysenteriae, Shigella flexneri, Shigella boydii, and Shigella sonnei are the four species associated with this condition."
    },
