    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "When branches of the trigeminal nerve are involved, lesions may appear on the face, in the mouth, in the eye, or on the tongue. Zoster ophthalmicus is usually a debilitating condition that can result in blindness in the absence of antiviral therapy. In Ramsay Hunt syndrome, pain and vesicles appear in the external auditory canal, and patients lose their sense of taste in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue while developing ipsilateral facial palsy. The geniculate ganglion of the sensory branch of the facial nerve is involved.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "While rare, Herpes Zoster can lead to viral encephalitis, but it's not primarily associated with trigeminal nerve involvement. Encephalitis is more commonly associated with VZV primary infection.",
      "B": "Herpes Zoster is not a common cause of transverse myelitis. However, it can cause neurological complications, such as myelitis, in some cases.",
      "C": "Trigeminal nerve involvement in Herpes Zoster can lead to radiculoneuritis, causing pain, vesicles, and other sensory disturbances in the affected dermatomes.",
      "D": "When branches of the trigeminal nerve are involved, lesions may appear on the face, in the mouth, in the eye, or on the tongue. Zoster ophthalmicus is usually a debilitating condition that can result in blindness in the absence of antiviral therapy. In Ramsay Hunt syndrome, pain and vesicles appear in the external auditory canal, and patients lose their sense of taste in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue while developing ipsilateral facial palsy. The geniculate ganglion of the sensory branch of the facial nerve is involved."
    },
