    "explanation": "Herpes zoster is characterized by a unilateral vesicular dermatomal eruption, often associated with severe pain. The dermatomes from T3 to L3 are most frequently involved. If the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve is involved, zoster ophthalmicus results. Zoster ophthalmicus is usually a debilitating condition that can result in blindness in the absence of antiviral therapy.\nIn Ramsay Hunt syndrome, pain and vesicles appear in the external auditory canal, and patients lose their sense of taste in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue while developing ipsilateral facial palsy.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus primarily involves the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve, affecting the cornea, conjunctiva, and surrounding tissues. While severe cases may lead to vision impairment, it doesn't directly afflict the optic nerve.",
      "B": "The symptoms of Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus include pain, redness, and vesicular eruptions on the forehead, eyelids, and tip of the nose. However, a \"sandlike\" feeling inside the eye is not a common description.",
      "C": "While tearing or excessive tearing (epiphora) can occur due to irritation, tearing itself is not a direct danger. The more critical concern is the potential for corneal involvement and subsequent vision issues.",
      "D": "Herpes zoster is characterized by a unilateral vesicular dermatomal eruption, often associated with severe pain. The dermatomes from T3 to L3 are most frequently involved. If the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve is involved, zoster ophthalmicus results. Zoster ophthalmicus is usually a debilitating condition that can result in blindness in the absence of antiviral therapy.\nIn Ramsay Hunt syndrome, pain and vesicles appear in the external auditory canal, and patients lose their sense of taste in the anterior two-thirds of the tongue while developing ipsilateral facial palsy."
    },
