    "id": "notion-pathology-0074",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 74,
    "stem": "Situation 7 - During annual school physical examination, 17 years old Greg is observed to have abnormally long legs, small atrophic testes with small penis and lack of deep voice, facial hair and male pubic hair distribution. The doctor is thinking more along the lines of Klinefelter syndrome. What is the chromosomal abnormality for the above condition?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "1 X chromosome and no Y chromosome"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "2 or more X chromosomes and 1 or more Y chromosomes"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "1 X chromosome and 1 Y chromosome"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "2X chromosomes and no Y chromosome"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Klinefelter syndrome\nOccurs when there are at least two X\nchromosomes and one or more Y\nchromosomes\nMost often, the karyotype 47, XXY is\ncharacteristic. Variants include additional\nX chromosomes (e.g., XXXY) and rare\nmosaic forms",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Klinefelter syndrome is characterized by the presence of at least one extra X chromosome in males, typically resulting in a karyotype of 47,XXY.\nTurner syndrome\nDisorder that occurs when there is\ncomplete or partial monosomy of the X\nchromosome.\nAn Xo karyotype (45, X) is characteristic of Turner syndrome",
      "B": "Klinefelter syndrome\nOccurs when there are at least two X\nchromosomes and one or more Y\nchromosomes\nMost often, the karyotype 47, XXY is\ncharacteristic. Variants include additional\nX chromosomes (e.g., XXXY) and rare\nmosaic forms",
      "C": "This combination represents a normal male karyotype (46,XY).",
      "D": "This combination is not characteristic of Klinefelter syndrome. Klinefelter syndrome involves the presence of at least one Y chromosome along with additional X chromosomes."
    },
