    "correctChoice": "C",
    "explanation": "Antagonism of dopamine in the nigrostriatal tract is known to lead to Parkinson-like symptoms. This tract is associated with motor function, and disruption can result in extrapyramidal side effects such as tremors, rigidity, and bradykinesia.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "Antagonism of dopamine in the mesolimbic tract is associated with alleviating positive symptoms of schizophrenia, including hallucinations. It does not typically lead to Parkinson-like symptoms.",
      "B": "Antagonism of dopamine in the mesocortical tract is linked to addressing cognitive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia. It does not contribute to Parkinson-like symptoms.",
      "C": "Antagonism of dopamine in the nigrostriatal tract is known to lead to Parkinson-like symptoms. This tract is associated with motor function, and disruption can result in extrapyramidal side effects such as tremors, rigidity, and bradykinesia.",
      "D": "Antagonism of dopamine in the tuberoinfundibular tract can lead to increased levels of prolactin, causing side effects such as gynecomastia. It is not directly associated with Parkinson-like symptoms."
    },
