    "id": "notion-pathology-0098",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 98,
    "stem": "Situation 15- Aling Nilda comes to your clinic worried of a rapidly enlarging mass last month. She claims to have first noted the mass last month. Mass grew from the size of a corn kernel to its protruding size of a calamansi. You are considering Phyllodes tumor for this case. Which of the following statements MOST likely explains the bulbous protrusions commonly present in larger forms of this tumor? Presence of nodules of .",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Proliferating stroma covered by epithelium"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Normal stroma covered by distorted epithelium"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Proliferating stroma covered by distorted epithelium"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Normal stroma covered by proliferating epithelium"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "A",
    "explanation": "MOST LIKELY EXPLANATION: Phyllodes tumors are characterized by proliferating stroma, and the bulbous protrusions seen in larger forms are often due to the proliferation of stromal elements. The stroma may compress and distort the surrounding epithelium.\nThe tumors range in size from a few centimeters to massive lesions involving the entire breast. The larger lesions often have bulbous protrusions (phyllodes is Greek for “leaflike”) due to the presence of nodules of proliferating stroma covered by epithelium.",
    "choiceRationales": {
      "A": "MOST LIKELY EXPLANATION: Phyllodes tumors are characterized by proliferating stroma, and the bulbous protrusions seen in larger forms are often due to the proliferation of stromal elements. The stroma may compress and distort the surrounding epithelium.\nThe tumors range in size from a few centimeters to massive lesions involving the entire breast. The larger lesions often have bulbous protrusions (phyllodes is Greek for “leaflike”) due to the presence of nodules of proliferating stroma covered by epithelium.",
      "B": "LESS LIKELY: Phyllodes tumors are more associated with proliferating stroma rather than normal stroma. The epithelium is often distorted due to the proliferating stromal cells.",
      "C": "ALSO PLAUSIBLE: This choice is similar to option (a) and is consistent with the characteristic features of Phyllodes tumors.",
      "D": "LESS LIKELY: Phyllodes tumors are primarily characterized by the proliferation of stromal cells rather than epithelial cells."
    },
