    "id": "superexam-pathology-0013",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 13,
    "stem": "Patient sought consult for chest pain and\nhemoptysis associated with hematuria, and\nsigns and symptoms of uremia. His blood\npressure is elevated and he has grade III\nbipedal edema. As the clinician in charge, you\nsuspect that he is suffering from an\nautoimmune disease in which antibodies\nagainst type IV collagen attack the basement\nmembrane of the lungs and kidneys. On\nimmunofluorescence renal biopsy would most\nprobably reveal:",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Granular IgG and C3 in GBM and mesangium"
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "Linear IgG and C3"
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Negative"
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Focal IgM or C3"
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "IgA in the mesangium"
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "B",
    "explanation": "Don’t just focus on the electron and LM findings and\nsacrifice immunofluorescence. SIMILAR TO PREVIOUS\nBOARD EXAM CONCEPT/PRINCIPLE.\nA rule of thumb is if the pathophysiology involves\nimmune complex deposition, it would usually present\nwith a granular pattern. If the pathophysiology\ninvolves antibodies against basement membranes, the\npattern is usually linear.\nThis is actually a case of Goodpasture syndrome which\ninvolves antibodies against the basement membrane\nof the lungs and the kidneys and the findings on\nimmunofluorescent studies show linear IgG and C3.\nSource: Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of\nDisease 8th ed p. 709, 918",
