    "id": "superexam-pathology-0039",
    "subject": "pathology",
    "originalNumber": 39,
    "stem": "A 40-year-old woman presents with a painless\nmass anterior to her left ear. The mass had\nbeen slowly enlarging over the past year. The\nmass is firm and nontender. Computed\ntomography and magnetic resonance imaging\nreveal a well-circumscribed, homogeneous\nmass within the left parotid gland. Biopsy\nreveals anastomosing strands of stellate and\nfusiform epithelial cells embedded in a myxoid\nstroma. Which of the following is a\ncharacteristic of the lesion?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "It is also called papillary cystadenoma\nlymphomatosum."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "It is most often localized to the\nsubmandibular gland."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "It is the most common malignant salivary\ngland tumor."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Recurrence often takes place after surgical\nresection."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Surgical resection should not be performed,\nbecause this condition is usually already\nmetastatic on diagnosis."
      }
    ],
    "correctChoice": "D",
    "explanation": "Approximately 80% to 90% of salivary gland tumors\noriginate in the parotid gland and, of these,\napproximately 70% are pleomorphic adenomas. The\nterm “mixed tumor” properly applies to this benign\ntumor, which often demonstrates myxoid and\ncartilage-like elements in addition to stellate or\nfusiform epithelial cells. Complete surgical resection is\ndifficult because of the tumor’s proximity to the facial\nnerve, and, thus, recurrence is frequent.",
